{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789483643,"nonce":"DjE+1twTBnFcKQd9/QFYhTJXP1A=","title":"The optimizer pays whole rounds for the folders, and the inliner re-walks the caller per splice","message":"Three costs in the optimizer's fixed point, measured on the M1 Pro with `ZYNTAX_TRACE_OPT_PHASES=1`:\n\n1. After the first round of bench_tokenize, sign_fold rewrote three compares, const_fold folded them a round later, and a third round confirmed nothing moved; each of those rounds ran every pass, about 2 ms each. Now when a round's only progress is the folders' (const_fold, sign_fold, cse), they are iterated to their own fixed point within the round and the outer loop stops.\n\n2. `MAX_INLINES_PER_CALLER_PER_ROUND` was 8, so bench_pipeline's `main` took seven inner rounds for its 49 inlines, each round re-deriving loops, cold paths and the instruction count; about 1 ms a round. The caller-size budgets bound the work regardless, so the cap is now 64.\n\n3. Every splice rebuilt the call's block by cloning each instruction, walked the whole function to substitute the call's result, and `shift_remove`d the result from the value table (linear in the table); 40 to 170 us per inline of a one-instruction callee. Now a splice is `Vec::splice`, the substitutions of all of a function's splices are applied in one walk (chased, for a body that returned another call's result), the orphaned results leave the table in one `retain`, and the block edges are rebuilt once per function rather than per multi-block splice.\n\nOptimizer time: bench_tokenize 12.9 to 10.3 ms, bench_records 26.5 to 25, bench_pipeline 27.7 to 24. Trace: `[OPT] round N progress:` names what each round changed.\n\nRemaining in the optimizer on the data kernels: drop_insert's module facts (2.5 to 4.3 ms, recomputed over the library each run; the hooks a program generates make them program-dependent), the vectorizers' per-round analysis on loops that never vectorize (2.5 ms), and the inliner's per-round analyses (about 0.5 ms a round on a 500-instruction caller).","files":null}]}